Introduction
This report presents an Analytics Implementation Plan and Portfolio for Really Big Bank (RBB), focusing on the development of a reliable market intelligence analytics system. Using the Reserve Bank of Australia's Interbank Overnight Cash Rate and S&P/ASX 200 market data, the project outlines a structured approach to data ingestion, validation, transformation and management reporting. Supported by Python-based proofs of concept and interactive Power BI dashboards, the proposed solution demonstrates technical feasibility while emphasizing data quality, responsible AI utilization, analytical governance and effective decision-making.
Part 1: Data Pipeline Implementation Plan
1. Data Pipeline Implementation Plan
1.1 Data Source Selection
To monitor trends in the Australian market and to assist with setting performance benchmarks in the future, Really Big Bank (RBB) needs dependable financial data. The two sources chosen were the Reserve Bank of Australia's (RBA) daily Interbank Overnight Cash Rate and the closing prices of the S&P/ASX 200 index obtained via Yahoo Finance (Reserve Bank of Australia, n.d.; Yahoo Finance, n.d.). The cash rate gives information about the state of monetary conditions, whereas the ASX 200 shows the performance of the wider Australian equity market. Based on these indicators, RBB is able to examine the relationship between changes in market movements and shifts in interest rates.
1.2 Data Ingestion and Scheduling
Python was chosen for use in data ingestion because of its ease of use and suitability for carrying out repeated financial data processing. The requests library pulls the RBA's F1 Excel workbook directly, and yfinance is used to obtain the historical ASX 200 observations. A initial collection period of 120 calendar days was selected in order to include extra observations for the later rolling calculations.
The production pipeline suggested would be set to run automatically at 7:00 pm every weekday in Sydney, following the ASX trading session. A scheduling service, such as GitHub Actions, would trigger the ingestion scripts on weekdays, with execution configured to account for Sydney's daylight-saving time. For each execution, source availability and the freshness of the data would be checked, and any failed requests would be retried and logged for later investigation. Although the present proof of concept shows that retrieval is repeatable, automated scheduling is still only proposed as an implementation.
1.3 Storage and Pipeline Architecture
Figure 1 shows that the architecture includes ingestion, raw storage, validation, transformation and curated storage; original RBA workbooks are kept using timestamped filenames and the retrieved ASX 200 observations are stored as CSV snapshots. In production, version control is applied to both sources in order to preserve the historical retrievals. Retaining the original datasets also allows the bank to reprocess historical observations if calculation rules change or errors are discovered. 
Data validation takes place before transformation, during which the dates are standardized, the time-zone differences are dealt with and the financial metrics are calculated. The results of the validation are then stored separately for use in dashboard reporting. The fact that the data is stored separately serves to protect the integrity of the source, enables reproducibility and allows analysts to trace the results back to their original records.
 
