1.4 Error Handling and Data Quality
It is important to ensure that the data is reliable when it is being used as a basis for management decisions, and so the pipeline includes checks for missing values, invalid records, and failed downloads.
The testing identified 86 RBA observations within the selected analysis period (16 June to 13 October 2026), with no missing cash-rate values. One missing RBA cash-rate observation was identified on 14 October 2026, outside the selected analysis period. This issue was recorded in a separate data-quality log without inputting a replacement value.
The method of handling failures involved the use of a deliberately incorrect URL for the Reserve Bank of Australia (RBA). The Python script detected the HTTP 404 error that resulted and noted the timestamp, the source, and the error message in a CSV log file. These measures set up an auditable basis for identifying and investigating data-quality problems. Missing observations would be retained in the quality log and excluded from calculations requiring valid rates until investigated. The system would flag unavailable or outdated macroeconomic observations rather than treating them as newly verified data.
1.5 Proof of Concept and Leadership Handover
The proof of concept for Google Colab shows that it is possible to carry out retrieval, preserve the raw data, detect missing values, and log download errors. Even though the scope is limited, these functional elements prove that the proposed pipeline is technically feasible.
When carrying out the handover operation, the junior analyst would be given a documented repository, together with a data dictionary, the execution instructions and a validation checklist. To aid with maintenance, consistent naming conventions, code reviews and testing standards would be applied. Before being deployed, AI-assisted code would also need to be independently verified against the expected outputs in order to make sure that the efficiency gains do not affect data accuracy or accountability. Before approving changes, the analytics lead would require the junior analyst to demonstrate successful ingestion, verify calculated outputs against expected results, and confirm that errors were appropriately logged. 
Part 2: Data Curation Plan
2.1 Metric Definitions and Business Justification
Really Big Bank (RBB) uses the Reserve Bank of Australia’s (RBA) Interbank Overnight Cash Rate combined with S&P/ASX 200 closing prices to benchmark performance and track market trends. Three metrics were implemented using the pandas library in Python:
•	Moving Average (MA_10): The rolling(10, min_periods=10) average of the last 10 ASX trading-day closing prices.average() It averages out short-term fluctuations to determine longer-term market trends.
•	Daily Percentage Return – Percentage change between consecutive closing prices (i.e. Daily Percentage Return = pct_change(fill_method=None) * 100). It tracks market performance on a daily basis. 
•	14-Day Volatility: Sample standard deviation of fourteen daily returns, calculated using rolling (14, min_periods=14).std(ddof=1). It is expressed in percentage points and helps identify market uncertainty.
These metrics allow for repeatable financial monitoring. RBA rates provide monetary policy context, not causation. 
 
