2.2 Data Alignment and Time Zone Handling 
The datasets were standardized and aligned using pandas. RBA Excel dates were converted to calendar dates and ASX timestamps were parsed as UTC and converted to Australia/Sydney by tz_convert(), reduced to Sydney dates considering the daylight saving.
ASX trading dates set the common analysis period (16 June-13 October 2026). Thus, RBA records were filtered and both sources were aligned through a left join (pd.merge()). validate="one_to_one" and indicator=True checked matching integrity.
The results confirmed 86 ASX observations, 86 successful matches, 0 unmatched dates and 0 missing RBA rates. 
 
Figure 2. Data alignment validation results.
We avoid artificial weekend observations by using trading dates. But a matching date does not tell you when the RBA information was available to the public. When the information is published it has to be checked before using it in real-time or for prediction. 
2.3 Data Quality Management
Automated checks were conducted for missing values, duplicate dates and invalid ASX closing prices. A missing RBA cash rate observation was located on 14 October 2026, beyond the selected analysis period. It was kept in logs/part2_data_quality_log.csv instead of being replaced by an unsupported estimate.
No RBA rates were missing, and no ASX closing prices were invalid in the matched dataset. Nine for MA_10, one for Daily Return and fourteen for Volatility_14, were deliberately left as null values, due to not enough historic observations and not due to source errors. 
 
